{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS docs on Comprehend service role trust policy requirements"}

{"id": "b32eac8d-6ca5-4ad5-b341-38defe00377e", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "This confirms it. Here's the chain:

1. **Caller → `comprehend:StartEntitiesDetectionJob`** — allowed (caller has the permission).
2. **Caller → `iam:PassRole` on the role** — allowed (caller has the permission).
3. **`comprehend.amazonaws.com` → `sts:AssumeRole` on the role** — this is the denying hop. Comprehend's service itself must be allowed to assume the role via the role's **trust policy** (`AssumeRolePolicyDocument`). A role that works for Textract has a trust policy listing `textract.amazonaws.com` as the trusted principal — but if `comprehend.amazonaws.com` isn't also listed there, Comprehend's attempt to assume the role fails before it ever gets to S3 input/output access.

That's why the same role "works" for Textract (which assumes it fine) but throws `AccessDeniedException` for Comprehend even though your caller identity has both permissions needed on the caller side — the caller's permissions are irrelevant to this hop; it's a service-principal trust check, not a caller-identity check.

**Fix:** update the role's trust policy to add `comprehend.amazonaws.com` as an additional trusted principal:

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    { "Effect": "Allow", "Principal": { "Service": "textract.amazonaws.com" }, "Action": "sts:AssumeRole" },
    { "Effect": "Allow", "Principal": { "Service": "comprehend.amazonaws.com" }, "Action": "sts:AssumeRole" }
  ]
}
```

If you want, I can check the role's actual trust policy and permissions policy in your account to confirm this is the gap.", "type": "text"}], "created_at": null}

